---
title: Status
order: 3
description: rc-status, dependency info
tags: [openrc, rc-status, status, dependencies]
---

rc-status shows the state of services across runlevels and their dependency relationships.

@text
Viewing Status

@code bash
# Show all services in the current runlevel with status
rc-status

# Show all runlevels and their services
rc-status -a

# Show only crashed/stopped services
rc-status -c

# Show services in a specific runlevel
rc-status -r default
rc-status -r boot

# Show manually started services (not in any runlevel)
rc-status -m

# Show unused services (not started, not in any runlevel)
rc-status -u

@text
Service States

@code bash
# Possible states shown by rc-status:
# [ started  ]   — service is running
# [ stopped  ]   — service is not running
# [ crashed  ]   — service was running but is no longer (process died)
# [ starting ]   — service is in the process of starting
# [ stopping ]   — service is in the process of stopping
# [ inactive ]   — service is not enabled in any runlevel

@text
Dependency Information

@code bash
# Show dependency tree for a specific service
rc-service sshd describe

# List what a service needs, uses, and provides
grep -A20 "^depend" /etc/init.d/sshd

# Common dependency keywords in init scripts:
# need  — hard dependency, must be running (fails if dep fails)
# use   — soft dependency, start if available (won't fail)
# after — ordering only, start after this service
# before — ordering only, start before this service
# provide — virtual service name this script provides
# keyword — special flags (nonet, nojail, etc.)

# Example depend() block:
# depend() {
#     need net
#     use dns logger
#     after firewall
# }

# Check service dependency tree with rc-service
rc-service sshd describe

@text
Practical Status Checks

@code bash
# Quick check: is anything crashed?
rc-status -c
# Empty output means all services in their expected state

# Find services that are started but not in any runlevel
rc-status -m

# Overview of system state
rc-status -a 2>/dev/null | grep -E "started|stopped|crashed"

# Count running services
rc-status | grep -c "started"

# Find which runlevel a service belongs to
rc-update show | grep sshd
